# produces exactly what the plain .sub produces and its output is UNTAGGED --
# the SELECTION_TAG only records deviations from the default. Kept so the
# submission recipe stays greppable, but prefer the plain .sub (which also
# supports sharding). Delete when you are sure nothing references it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# export_training_data_loose.sub — training export with the VBS topology cut OFF.
#
# Identical to export_training_data.sub except for `--vbs-deta=0`, which drops
# the VBS candidate-pair |Deta| >= 3.0 requirement while KEEPING everything else,
# including MIN_PASSETA_JETS = 1 (so it is still a forward-jet event, just not one
# forced into a VBS topology).
#
# Why: on Z+jets that cut removed 87% of everything surviving the lepton
# selection (520,126 -> 67,184 out of 3M). Worse, it has no truth-HS matching, so
# at this pileup level it preferentially keeps events whose "VBS pair" involves a
# PILEUP jet -- a genuine Z+jets event rarely supplies two >30 GeV jets in
# opposite hemispheres at |Deta| >= 3 on its own. That is exactly the pathology
# the timing study is trying to measure, so the cut may have been manufacturing
# the effect rather than exposing it. Expect roughly 7.7x more Z+jets events.
#
# Output names are tagged by resolveSelection(), so these land as
# <sample>/<sample>_training.root and CANNOT overwrite the standard
# export. Both selections can be loaded side by side in training.ipynb.
#
# Submit (from inside condor/):
#   cd build && cmake .. && make export_training_data   # build BEFORE submitting
#   cd ../condor && condor_submit export_training_data_loose.sub
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
universe                 = vanilla
executable               = run_analysis.sh

# run_analysis.sh takes <executable> <sample> [<threads>] [<max-events>] [extra...].
# Threads and max-events are simply omitted (this executable is a plain sequential
# TTreeReader loop, so request_cpus stays 1); trailing --flags are classified by
# shape by run_analysis.sh, so no empty positional placeholders are needed.
arguments                = export_training_data $(sample) --vbs-deta=0

should_transfer_files    = YES
when_to_transfer_output  = ON_EXIT
transfer_input_files     = ../build/export_training_data
transfer_output_files    = $(sample)/$(sample)_training.root

# Same remap rationale as the other .sub files: condor flattens a nested
# transfer_output_files path to its basename when staging back, so the remap is
# what makes the file land under $(sample)/ on the submit side. The LHS must be a
# BARE FILENAME -- per condor_submit(1), "You cannot specify directories to be
# remapped" -- while directories are allowed on the RHS and created as needed.
transfer_output_remaps   = "$(sample)_training.root=$(sample)/$(sample)_training.root"

getenv                   = True

+JobFlavour              = "tomorrow"

# Sequential job. Memory/disk are raised above the standard export because the
# loosened selection keeps several times more events, and the per-track tree
# dominates the output size.
request_cpus             = 1
request_memory           = 6GB
request_disk             = 24GB

output                   = logs/export_loose_$(sample).$(ClusterId).$(ProcId).out
error                    = logs/export_loose_$(sample).$(ClusterId).$(ProcId).err
log                      = logs/export_loose_$(sample).$(ClusterId).$(ProcId).log

queue sample in (zjets)
